{ "source": "// SPDX-License-Identifier: MIT
pragma solidity >=0.8.0;

contract DecompiledContract {
    uint256 public count;

    function Unresolved_2a1afcd9() public view returns (bytes memory) {
        return _readStorageBytes(1);
    }

    function Unresolved_4ed3885e(uint256 arg0) public {
        count = 0x01 + (arg0 + arg0);
    }

    function Unresolved_6d4ce63c() public view returns (bytes memory) {
        return _readStorageBytes(1);
    }

    function _readStorageBytes(uint256 slot) internal view returns (bytes memory) {
        uint256 length;
        uint256 data;
        assembly {
            let packed := sload(slot)
            switch and(packed, 0x1)
            case 0 {
                length := sload(add(slot, 0x1))
                data := sload(add(slot, 0x2))
            }
            default {
                length := shr(0x1, packed)
                data := shr(0x8, packed)
            }
        }
        if ((0x1f & length) == 0) {
            return abi.encodePacked(bytes32(0x20), bytes32(length));
        } else {
            uint256 mask = ~(0x0100 ** (0x20 - (0x1f & length)) - 0x01);
            return abi.encodePacked(bytes32(0x20), bytes32(length), bytes32(mask & data));
        }
    }
}" ,"notes":"Fixed the assembly `if ... else` (inline assembly `if` has no else branch) by rewriting it as a `switch` statement. Also replaced `!(0x1f & length)` with an explicit `== 0` comparison, since Solidity's `!` operator only accepts `bool`, and made the `abi.encodePacked` arguments explicitly typed as `bytes32` to avoid literal-type ambiguity."}

message.reasoning_content:
The issue: `if and(packed, 0x1) { ... } else { ... }` — assembly if doesn't support else. Convert to if/else statement properly: use `switch` or restructure. Also `0x0100 ** ...` in assembly? No, that's outside assembly — fine but `~(...)` on uint256 fine. Actually the else inside assembly is the error. Fix: use switch or two ifs.

Also `abi.encodePacked(0x20, length)` returns bytes memory — fine? encodePacked returns bytes memory, yes.

Fix assembly:

```
assembly {
    let packed := sload(slot)
    switch and(packed, 0x1)
    case 0 {
        length := sload(add(slot, 0x1))
        data := sload(add(slot, 0x2))
    }
    default {
        length := shr(0x1, packed)
        data := shr(0x8, packed)
    }
}
```

Also `0x0100 ** (0x20 - (0x1f & length))` — exponent types: 0x20 - ... literal ok. `~(...) & data` — ~ of uint256 fine? The expression `0x0100 ** (...) - 0x01` type: literal power... may need cast. `~(x)` where x is uint256? 0x0100**... literal arithmetic yields uint256? Actually literal**literal stays rational? Might complain. Safer: compute mask as uint256. But minimal: keep, likely compiles since length is uint256 making expression uint256. `0x1f & length` uint256, so fine.

Also `if (!(0x1f & length))` — `!` on uint256 invalid! `!` requires bool. Fix: `if ((0x1f & length) == 0)`.
